{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-95982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{x}^{3}y=5{y}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-95982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ which has order $2.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-95982" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12cc794d0be2ef88bc935bf87f761f03ca15512d817ed3733ccab3d5ee5c2f7e"} {"question" "q-95982" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-95982" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-95982" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-{y}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}={y}^{5}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ which has order $4.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12cc794d0be2ef88bc935bf87f761f03ca15512d817ed3733ccab3d5ee5c2f7e"} {"question" "q-118781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are second-order ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{″}{x}^{3}-2y{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\n- $3{y}^{″}{y}^{′}-2y=2{y}^{′}$\n- $4{y}^{‴}x-7{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y{e}^{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is of second-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\n- Equation II is also a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\n- Equation III is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12cc794d0be2ef88bc935bf87f761f03ca15512d817ed3733ccab3d5ee5c2f7e"} {"question" "q-96672" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96672" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96672" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}-4{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ which has order $4.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12cc794d0be2ef88bc935bf87f761f03ca15512d817ed3733ccab3d5ee5c2f7e"} {"question" "q-96663" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96663" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96663" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}+3x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}={x}^{8}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}},$ which has order $6.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12cc794d0be2ef88bc935bf87f761f03ca15512d817ed3733ccab3d5ee5c2f7e"} {"question" "q-118779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12cc794d0be2ef88bc935bf87f761f03ca15512d817ed3733ccab3d5ee5c2f7e"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+{y}^{2}={x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12cc794d0be2ef88bc935bf87f761f03ca15512d817ed3733ccab3d5ee5c2f7e"} {"question" "q-118869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${e}^{-y}{y}^{′}=-\\sqrt{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}-{y}^{3}=2{e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}=3\\sqrt{y}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y}{y}^{′} & =-\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\sqrt{y}{e}^{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-\\sqrt{y}{e}^{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{y}^{3} & =2{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={y}^{3}+2{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={y}^{3}+2{e}^{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}} & =3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12cc794d0be2ef88bc935bf87f761f03ca15512d817ed3733ccab3d5ee5c2f7e"} {"question" "q-96713" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96713" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96713" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={e}^{3-y}-2$\n- $y{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=1$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+y)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{3-y}-2.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12cc794d0be2ef88bc935bf87f761f03ca15512d817ed3733ccab3d5ee5c2f7e"} {"question" "q-118870" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118870" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118870" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\sqrt{2y}$\n- ${y}^{′}-{y}^{3}=3\\sqrt{y}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\sqrt{2y}.$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{y}^{3} & =3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={y}^{3}+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={y}^{3}+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12cc794d0be2ef88bc935bf87f761f03ca15512d817ed3733ccab3d5ee5c2f7e"} {"question" "q-96715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′},\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$\n- $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=2$\n- $4{y}^{′}-\\frac{1}{3}xy=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96986" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12cc794d0be2ef88bc935bf87f761f03ca15512d817ed3733ccab3d5ee5c2f7e"} {"question" "q-96986" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96986" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-96986" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x},\\,y(1)=6$\n- $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(4)=-3$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12cc794d0be2ef88bc935bf87f761f03ca15512d817ed3733ccab3d5ee5c2f7e"} {"question" "q-118791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13