I is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2x{y}^{″}={y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-95985" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12cc794d0be2ef88bc935bf87f761f03ca15512d817ed3733ccab3d5ee5c2f7e"} {"question" "q-95985" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-95985" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-95985" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118790" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-9x,\\,y(1)=-4$\n- ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y,\\,y(0)=-3$\n- ${y}^{″}+y=3$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118790" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}=-9x$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=-4.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=-3.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118790" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12cc794d0be2ef88bc935bf87f761f03ca15512d817ed3733ccab3d5ee5c2f7e"} {"question" "q-118790" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118790" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118790" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-x=-7y,\\,y(2)=3$\n- ${x}^{4}{y}^{‴}+3y={2}^{y},\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{″}(0)=0$\n- $x{y}^{″}-6y=14,\\,y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}-x=-7y$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${x}^{4}{y}^{‴}+3y={2}^{y}$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1,$ and ${y}^{″}(0)=0.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $x{y}^{″}-6y=14$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12cc794d0be2ef88bc935bf87f761f03ca15512d817ed3733ccab3d5ee5c2f7e"} {"question" "q-118787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "q-118787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "0fcbb7692a60bcba79ec1c9dd37e5f7c01f6be1abc8a0a8e01464818b8bdfbaa"} {"question" "e-8459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $2x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}+y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}=3x^2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/example-8459.json" "file_sha256" "296788228679c99975543037b8c07a110abb9645b65095ff5a1deb403a020e7b"} {"question" "e-8459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3},$ which has order $3.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/example-8459.json" "file_sha256" "296788228679c99975543037b8c07a110abb9645b65095ff5a1deb403a020e7b"} {"question" "e-8460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n\n- $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/example-8460.json" "file_sha256" "b5afa99cecbe66f86cb0d15aae4e9cad338835a33489e7cbad2da8a3786c42b6"} {"question" "e-8460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, $y'=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)= y^3.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{y}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}+2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)= \\dfrac{y^3+2}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)= 1+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/example-8460.json" "file_sha256" "b5afa99cecbe66f86cb0d15aae4e9cad338835a33489e7cbad2da8a3786c42b6"} {"question" "e-8461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2-3y' = y^3, \\quad y(1)=6$\n\n- $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}, \\quad y(2)=e$\n\n- $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y', \\quad y(2)=3, \\quad y'(2) = 2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/example-8461.json" "file_sha256" "6834e5536311eec63e9df52f7815519de9d7b0327f9535b2feb28e87a2344b60"} {"question" "e-8461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\quad y(x_0) = y_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^2-3y' = y^3$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System II is also a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=e.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y'$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=3,\\, y'(2) = 2.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/example-8461.json" "file_sha256" "6834e5536311eec63e9df52f7815519de9d7b0327f9535b2feb28e87a2344b60"}] "usage" {"q-95982" [] "e-8461" [] "e-8459" [] "q-96986" [] "e-8460" [] "q-95985" []} "basis" 869 "retractions" [[17592186167377 :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + x^{3}y = 5y^{4}\\frac{dy}{dx}$?"] [17592186167377 :question/worked-solution "The highest derivative appearing is $d^2y/dx^2$. The powers $x^3$ and $y^4$ do not determine the order. Therefore the ODE has order **2**."] [17592186167437 :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n1. $\\frac{1}{2}x^{2}y^{\\prime \\prime } = 3y^{\\prime }, y(1) = 2, y^{\\prime }(1) = 1$\n2. $2x^{2}y^{\\prime } = y, y(0) = 2$\n3. $4y^{\\prime } - \\frac{1}{3}xy = \\ln y$"] [17592186167437 :question/worked-solution "System I is second order because it contains $y''$. System II is a first-order equation with one initial condition. System III has no initial condition. Thus **II only** has the form of a first-order IVP.\n\nSystem II is inconsistent at its initial point: $2(0)^2y'(0)=y(0)$ would require $0=2$. An IVP may be posed without having a solution."] [17592186167380 :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n1. $2xy^{\\prime } = y, y(0) = 1$\n2. $y^{\\prime } - 2xy = e^{y}$\n3. $2xy^{\\prime \\prime } = y^{\\prime }, y(1) = 1, y^{\\prime }(1) = 1$"] [17592186167380 :question/worked-solution "System I combines a first-order equation with a specified value $y(0)=1$, so it has the form of a first-order IVP. II has no initial condition. III contains $y''$ and is second order. Select **I only**.\n\nThe classification does not assert existence: system I has no differentiable solution satisfying the equation at $x=0$, because substituting its initial data gives $0=1$."] [17592186166583 :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $2x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}+y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}=3x^2$?"] [17592186166583 :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}\n$$\n\nwhich has order $3$.\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $3$."] [17592186166584 :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n2. $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n3. $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$"] [17592186166584 :question/worked-solution "A first-order equation is autonomous if it can be written as $y'=f(y)$ with no explicit dependence on $x$.\n\n- I is $y'=y^3$, so it is autonomous.\n- II rearranges to $y'=(y^3+2)/x$ for $x\\ne0$. Its right-hand side explicitly depends on $x$, so it is not autonomous.\n- III rearranges to $y'=1+3\\sqrt y$, which depends only on $y$, so it is autonomous.\n\nThe correct answer is **I and III only**."] [17592186166585 :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n1. $x^2-3y' = y^3, \\quad y(1)=6$\n2. $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}, \\quad y(2)=e$\n3. $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y', \\quad y(2)=3, \\quad y'(2) = 2$"] [17592186166585 :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\quad y(x_0) = y_0\n$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^2-3y' = y^3$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6$.\n- System II is also a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=e$.\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y'$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=3,\\, y'(2) = 2$. This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186165811 17592186165812 17592186165813 17592186165814 17592186165815 17592186165829 17592186165830 17592186165831 17592186165832 17592186165833 17592186166171 17592186166172 17592186166173 17592186166174 17592186166175] "immutable_field